In message <bo90hn$o2n$1@news-int.gatech.edu>, Bruce wrote:
> robin <news@unrated.net> deserves a cookie for saying:
..
> > The two ascension trophies were suggested by players as good measures
> > of NetHack skill.  We've always thought of the Tournament as having
> > two styles of trophy, the "standard" per-class trophies (only one of
> > which can go to any particular player) and the "special" trophies.
> 
> But isn't this, by nature, an arbitrary distinction?  You could just as
> easily decide that the overall ascension trophies are "standard" and the
> per-class tropies are "special."  Or that they're all "standard."  

Not really; NetHack itself is written to use just the scores as a way of
ranking players.  The "standard" ranking in NetHack is merely raw highest score
which, in order to have more trophies and reflect one aspect of the game's
complexity, we expanded to raw highest score sorted into classes.

The other three trophies were added to reflect other aspects of NetHack's
complexity.  In general, if we're going to add tropies, we'd prefer to add new
ones which will broaden the areas of the game reflected in the trophies.  Next
year, for example, we'll probably run the Tournament with the conducts
activated and start to add trophies for highest score with various conducts.

> There doesn't seem to be a compelling basis for ranking the per-class
> trophies and not the overall ascensions.  It just seems peculiar to
> reward something that anyone with a cockatrice corpse, stone to flesh,
> and a high tolerance for tedium could accomplish instead of something
> that actually takes significant skill.  

I dunno; I find the fact that the only ranking NetHack itself tracks is the raw
core to be a compelling argument for considering score-based rankings to be the
"standard" series.  ;-)

-robin


